// Generated by scripts/merge_contexts.py
export const contexts: Record<number, string> = {
  1: "Article 5 of the Basic Law (Grundgesetz) guarantees freedom of opinion (Meinungsfreiheit) to everyone in Germany. This means people can openly criticise the government, media, or any public figure without fear of punishment. It is one of the core civil liberties in German democracy.",
  2: "Religious education (Religionsunterricht) is a regular subject in German state schools, but parents have the right to opt their children out until the age of 14. After 14, children can decide for themselves whether to attend. This reflects Germany's principle of religious freedom alongside the state's role in education.",
  3: "A Rechtsstaat (rule-of-law state) means that both the government and all residents are bound by the law — no one is above it. This principle is enshrined in the Basic Law and prevents arbitrary exercise of state power. It is one of the five core principles of Germany's constitutional order.",
  4: "Freedom of opinion (Meinungsfreiheit) is guaranteed by Article 5 of the Grundgesetz as a fundamental right. Other options like Waffenbesitz (gun ownership) or Selbstjustiz (vigilante justice) are not fundamental rights. Germany's Basic Law lists a specific catalogue of Grundrechte in Articles 1–19.",
  5: "Free elections mean voters cannot be bribed, pressured, or punished for how they vote. The secrecy of the ballot protects every voter's independence. Germany's electoral principles are: universal, direct, free, equal, and secret (allgemein, unmittelbar, frei, gleich, geheim).",
  6: "Germany's constitution is called the Grundgesetz (Basic Law), adopted on 23 May 1949. It was originally intended as a provisional document for West Germany, but became the permanent constitution after reunification in 1990. It begins with the famous line: 'Die Würde des Menschen ist unantastbar.'",
  7: "Freedom of faith and conscience (Glaubens- und Gewissensfreiheit) is guaranteed by Article 4 of the Grundgesetz. This protects both religious belief and the freedom to have no religion at all. The other options — entertainment, work, housing — are not listed as fundamental rights in the Basic Law.",
  8: "The Grundgesetz guarantees dignity, equality before the law, and freedom of opinion — but it does not require equal wealth distribution. The idea that 'everyone should have the same amount of money' reflects socialist economic policy, not a constitutional right. Germany has a social market economy, not a planned one.",
  9: "The right to asylum (Asylrecht) is unique among fundamental rights in the Grundgesetz because it applies only to foreign nationals, not to German citizens. It is guaranteed by Article 16a. All other fundamental rights like human dignity and freedom of opinion apply to everyone regardless of nationality.",
  10: "The Grundgesetz is compatible with financial penalties (Geldstrafe) as a legal punishment. However, it explicitly prohibits torture, corporal punishment (Prügelstrafe), and the death penalty. Article 102 abolished the death penalty in Germany when the Basic Law was adopted in 1949.",
  11: "Germany's constitution is called the Grundgesetz, not Bundesverfassung or Verfassungsvertrag. The name 'Basic Law' was chosen deliberately in 1949 to signal that it was a temporary arrangement pending German reunification. After reunification in 1990, the name was kept out of tradition.",
  12: "Fundamental rights (Grundrechte) in the Basic Law are protected against abolition even by parliament. Article 79(3) — the 'eternity clause' (Ewigkeitsklausel) — forbids any changes to core principles including human dignity and the federal democratic structure. Press freedom cannot be abolished by any majority.",
  13: "The opposition consists of all members of parliament who are not part of the governing coalition. Their constitutional role is to scrutinise and challenge the government. In Germany, even parties that passed the 5% threshold but are not in government form the opposition.",
  14: "Freedom of opinion allows you to express views online, in print, or in public. However, it does not protect insults, deliberate false claims about individuals, or symbols of banned organisations like the Nazi swastika or ISIS. The internet is explicitly covered by Article 5's protection.",
  15: "Article 12(2) of the Grundgesetz explicitly prohibits forced labour (Zwangsarbeit). Germany's history of using forced labour under the Nazi regime made this prohibition especially important in 1949. Free choice of occupation (freie Berufswahl) is actually a protected right, not a prohibition.",
  16: "Freedom of opinion is limited when someone publicly spreads false factual claims about an identifiable person — this can constitute defamation (Verleumdung or üble Nachrede) under German law. Criticism of the government, discussions about religion, and criticism of the state are all protected speech.",
  17: "The General Equal Treatment Act (Allgemeines Gleichbehandlungsgesetz, AGG) and Article 3 of the Grundgesetz prohibit unequal treatment of citizens by the state. Citizens retain full rights to freedom of opinion, the right to petition, and freedom of assembly — these are protected, not prohibited.",
  18: "Article 1(1) of the Grundgesetz opens with 'Die Würde des Menschen ist unantastbar' — Human dignity is inviolable. This is deliberately placed first because it is the foundation of all other rights. It is part of the 'eternity clause' and cannot be amended even by a two-thirds parliamentary majority.",
  19: "Freizügigkeit (freedom of movement) is guaranteed by Article 11 of the Grundgesetz and means every person in Germany may choose their place of residence freely. It also includes the right to move between federal states without restriction. This is distinct from freedom of religion or freedom of profession.",
  20: "A party that aims to establish a dictatorship is unconstitutional (verfassungswidrig) because it violates the free democratic basic order (freiheitliche demokratische Grundordnung). Under Article 21(2) of the Grundgesetz, such parties can be banned by the Federal Constitutional Court. The KPD and SRP were banned on these grounds in the 1950s.",
  21: "The coat of arms of the Federal Republic of Germany shows a black eagle on a golden background — the Bundesadler. This heraldic eagle has been a symbol of German statehood since the medieval Holy Roman Empire. The current design was adopted in 1950.",
  22: "Germany is a republic, meaning the head of state is not a hereditary monarch but an elected official — the Federal President (Bundespräsident). Germany has been a republic since 1918 when the Kaiser abdicated. The Basic Law established it as a parliamentary republic.",
  23: "The majority of working people in Germany are employees (Arbeitnehmer) of companies or public authorities, not self-employed or volunteer workers. Germany has a large industrial and service sector with strong employee protections. Around 85% of the workforce is in dependent employment.",
  24: "Germany has exactly 16 federal states (Bundesländer). Three are city-states: Berlin, Hamburg, and Bremen. The other 13 are territorial states. Five of the 16 were formerly part of the GDR (East Germany) and joined the Federal Republic in 1990.",
  25: "Elsass-Lothringen (Alsace-Lorraine) is a region in northeastern France, not a German federal state. It was German territory between 1871 and 1918 and again briefly under Nazi occupation, but has been French since 1945. All other options — NRW, Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, Sachsen-Anhalt — are actual German states.",
  26: "Article 20 of the Grundgesetz defines Germany as a democratic and social federal state (demokratischer und sozialer Bundesstaat). This combines three principles: democracy, the welfare state (Sozialstaat), and federalism. Germany is neither communist nor a monarchy.",
  27: "Germany is a federal state (Bundesstaat), meaning power is constitutionally divided between the national government and 16 individual states. This is distinct from a centralised state (Zentralstaat) like France. Federalism is one of Germany's core constitutional principles protected by the eternity clause.",
  28: "The members of the Bundestag are directly elected by the eligible voting population (wahlberechtigtes Volk). Germany uses a mixed electoral system combining direct constituency votes (Erststimme) and party list votes (Zweitstimme). The military, business, or administration play no role in electing parliament.",
  29: "The eagle (Adler) has been the heraldic animal of German statehood for over a thousand years. The current Bundesadler — a black eagle on gold — was designed by Tobias Schwab and adopted in 1950. The bear is associated with Berlin specifically, not with the federal state.",
  30: "Press censorship (Pressezensur) is the opposite of a democratic feature — it is a hallmark of authoritarian regimes. Article 5 of the Grundgesetz explicitly prohibits censorship. Regular elections, freedom of opinion, and multiple parties are all genuine features of German democracy.",
  31: "A Koalition (coalition) is formed when no single party wins an outright majority, so two or more parties agree to govern together. Germany's proportional electoral system makes single-party majorities rare. Coalition agreements (Koalitionsverträge) set out the shared governing programme.",
  32: "Germany's state power is divided into three branches: the legislature (Gesetzgebung), the executive (Regierung/Exekutive), and the judiciary (Rechtsprechung). The press is not a branch of state power — it is independent and serves as a 'fourth estate' (vierte Gewalt) holding government to account.",
  33: "Germany has a secular constitution — church and state are formally separated. There is no state religion, and the government does not favour any particular faith. However, Germany does have a church tax system (Kirchensteuer) and religious education in schools, reflecting a cooperative rather than strictly separationist model.",
  34: "Germany is a republic with an elected head of state, so it is not a monarchy. It has been a republic since the abdication of Kaiser Wilhelm II in November 1918. Germany is simultaneously a democracy, a Rechtsstaat (rule-of-law state), and a Sozialstaat (welfare state).",
  35: "Germany's social insurance system (Sozialversicherung) is funded through Sozialabgaben — compulsory contributions paid by both employers and employees as a percentage of wages. This covers health, pension, unemployment, long-term care, and accident insurance. It is not funded by church tax or donations.",
  36: "Statutory health insurance (Krankenversicherung) is one of the five pillars of Germany's social security system and provides coverage for illness, hospitalisation, and preventive care. Car insurance, building insurance, and liability insurance are private matters and do not create social security in the same sense.",
  37: "The heads of government of Germany's 16 federal states are called Ministerpräsidentinnen/Ministerpräsidenten, except in the three city-states. Berlin's leader is the Regierende Bürgermeister/in, Hamburg's is the Erste Bürgermeister/in, and Bremen's is also the Erste Bürgermeister/in.",
  38: "Article 20(1) of the Grundgesetz states: 'Die Bundesrepublik Deutschland ist ein demokratischer und sozialer Bundesstaat.' This single sentence encodes three fundamental principles — democracy, the welfare state, and federalism — all protected by the eternity clause of Article 79(3).",
  39: "Each of Germany's 16 federal states has its own government (Landesregierung), parliament (Landtag or equivalent), and constitution. States have genuine legislative powers especially in education, policing, and cultural affairs. They do not have their own currency, army, or foreign ministry.",
  40: "The German national anthem uses the third stanza of the Lied der Deutschen, written by August Heinrich Hoffmann von Fallersleben in 1841. It begins 'Einigkeit und Recht und Freiheit' — Unity, Justice and Freedom. The first stanza ('Deutschland, Deutschland über alles') is not sung as it is associated with nationalism.",
  41: "Multiple parties exist in a democracy so that the full range of citizens' political opinions can be represented in parliament. Germany's proportional representation system means that parties winning over 5% of votes receive seats. This makes single-party rule unlikely and coalition-building necessary.",
  42: "In Germany's parliamentary system, new laws are passed by the Bundestag (parliament). The government can propose legislation, but it is parliament that formally debates and passes it. Courts interpret laws; the police enforce them; neither makes them.",
  43: "Under Article 21(2) of the Grundgesetz, a party can be banned if it seeks to undermine or abolish the free democratic basic order (freiheitliche demokratische Grundordnung). Only the Federal Constitutional Court (Bundesverfassungsgericht) can ban a party. High campaign costs, new ideas, or criticism of officials are not grounds for a ban.",
  44: "The Federal President (Bundespräsident) is not directly elected by the public but by the Bundesversammlung — a special assembly of all Bundestag members plus an equal number of representatives chosen by state parliaments. EU MEPs, state parliament members, and Bundestag members are all directly elected by voters.",
  45: "Long-term care insurance (Pflegeversicherung) is one of the five branches of Germany's statutory social insurance system, introduced in 1995. The others are health, pension, unemployment, and accident insurance. It covers costs of professional care for people who can no longer manage daily life independently.",
  46: "Building and maintaining roads, schools, and other public infrastructure is a core state responsibility in Germany. The state does not sell food or produce vehicles — those are private sector activities. Providing free newspapers to all residents is also not a state function.",
  47: "The German state pays child benefit (Kindergeld), supports museums, and promotes athletes — but it does not pay for holiday travel for all citizens. Social benefits are targeted at genuine needs, not lifestyle perks. This question tests understanding of the limits of state welfare provision.",
  48: "Germany's constitutional organs (Verfassungsorgane) are the Bundestag, Bundesrat, Federal President, Federal Government, and Federal Constitutional Court. A Bürgerversammlung (citizens' assembly) is not a constitutional organ — it is an informal participatory format used at local level, not established by the Basic Law.",
  49: "Education policy (Schulpolitik) in Germany is primarily a matter for the individual federal states (Bundesländer), not the federal government. This is known as Kulturhoheit der Länder — the cultural sovereignty of the states. This is why school systems, term dates, and curricula vary significantly between states.",
  50: "Germany's economic system is called the soziale Marktwirtschaft — the social market economy. It combines free market competition with social welfare protections and state regulation. The concept was developed by Ludwig Erhard and underpinned West Germany's post-war economic recovery, known as the Wirtschaftswunder.",
  51: "In a democratic rule-of-law state, arbitrary arrests by private forces without legal basis are not permitted — this is a feature of authoritarian states, not democracies. Citizens may criticise the government, demonstrate peacefully, and legal arrests following actual crimes are all consistent with the Rechtsstaat principle.",
  52: "Volkssouveränität (popular sovereignty) means that all state authority ultimately derives from the people, as stated in Article 20(2) of the Grundgesetz. Citizens exercise this power through elections and referendums. This principle distinguishes democracy from monarchy or dictatorship.",
  53: "A Rechtsstaat means the state itself must comply with the law — government action must have a legal basis and courts can strike down unlawful state acts. It does not mean the state is always right, nor that only right-wing parties exist, nor that citizens vote on every law.",
  54: "Germany's three branches of state power are the Legislative (parliament), Exekutive (government and administration), and Judikative (courts). 'Direktive' is not a recognised branch of state power in German constitutional law. The press is also not a state power — it is constitutionally independent.",
  55: "The image shows the Reichstagsgebäude in Berlin, which has been the seat of the German Bundestag since 1999 after its renovation by architect Sir Norman Foster, including the famous glass dome. The Federal Constitutional Court is in Karlsruhe, and the Chancellery is a separate building nearby.",
  56: "The Ordnungsamt (public order office) is a municipal authority responsible for enforcing local by-laws, parking regulations, noise complaints, and similar matters. The Finanzamt (tax office) is a state-level authority, the Auswärtiges Amt (Foreign Office) is federal, and a Pfarramt is a church parish office.",
  57: "By convention, the President of the Bundestag (Bundestagspräsident/in) is a member of the largest parliamentary group (stärkste Fraktion). Currently this is a CDU/CSU member. The role is the second-highest constitutional office in Germany after the Federal President.",
  58: "Under Article 64 of the Grundgesetz, federal ministers are formally appointed and dismissed by the Federal President, but only on the proposal of the Federal Chancellor. In practice, the Chancellor chooses the ministers and the President's role is ceremonial. The Bundesrat president and Bundestag president play no role.",
  59: "The first documented Jewish community on German soil dates back to around 321 CE — approximately 1,700 years ago — in the Roman city of Colonia (Cologne), based on a Roman edict. However, the question refers to 'about 1,150 years ago', reflecting communities documented around 900 CE. Jewish life has been present in Germany for well over a millennium.",
  60: "The Bundestag and Bundesrat both belong to the Legislative branch — they are the two chambers through which laws are made at the federal level. The Bundestag is the directly elected lower house; the Bundesrat represents the state governments. The executive and judiciary are separate branches.",
  61: "Volkssouveränität is the democratic principle that all legitimate state power originates with the people. Article 20(2) of the Grundgesetz states: 'Alle Staatsgewalt geht vom Volke aus.' This is exercised through elections and, in some cases, referendums at state level.",
  62: "When a state parliament (Landesparlament) is elected, it is called a Landtagswahl — a state parliament election. The equivalent national election is the Bundestagswahl. Local elections are Kommunalwahlen, and European Parliament elections are Europawahlen.",
  63: "The Exekutive (executive branch) includes the government, ministries, police, and administrative authorities like the Finanzamt. Courts belong to the Judikative (judiciary) — a separate, independent branch. This separation of powers ensures courts can check government actions without political interference.",
  64: "Germany has a three-tier administrative structure: the federal level (Bund), the 16 state governments (Länder), and municipalities (Kommunen — cities, towns and districts). This is not divided into occupation zones (a historical post-war structure) or cantons (the Swiss system).",
  65: "The Bundestag's main tasks are passing laws, electing the Chancellor, and scrutinising the government. Forming the cabinet (Bundeskabinett) is the Chancellor's responsibility — the Chancellor proposes ministers and the President formally appoints them. The Bundestag does not select or approve individual ministers.",
  66: "Berlin and Munich have the largest Jewish communities in Germany today, each with several thousand registered members. Berlin's Jewish community is the largest, reflecting the city's historical significance as a centre of Jewish culture before the Holocaust. Both communities have grown significantly since the 1990s with immigration from the former Soviet Union.",
  67: "Education policy (Schulpolitik) is the primary responsibility of Germany's federal states under the principle of Kulturhoheit der Länder. Defence and foreign affairs are federal responsibilities. This is why school holidays, curricula, and examination systems differ between states.",
  68: "Article 7(1) of the Grundgesetz states: 'Das gesamte Schulwesen steht unter der Aufsicht des Staates' — the entire school system is under state supervision. This is a direct constitutional mandate, not a practical choice. Private schools exist but are also subject to state oversight.",
  69: "Germany's administrative structure has three tiers: federal (Bund), state (Länder), and at the bottom, municipalities (Gemeinden) — which include cities, towns, and rural communities. Gemeinden are responsible for local services like public parks, local roads, and community centres. Stadträte and Landräte are officials within this tier, not the tier itself.",
  70: "The Federal President nominates the candidate for Federal Chancellor to the Bundestag for election, as set out in Article 63 of the Grundgesetz. The President does not govern, make laws, or select ministers independently. The role is largely ceremonial but symbolically important.",
  71: "The Federal Chancellor's main workplace is the Bundeskanzleramt (Federal Chancellery) in Berlin, close to the Reichstag where the Bundestag meets. Since the government moved from Bonn to Berlin in 1999, Berlin has been the primary seat of government. Schloss Bellevue is the President's residence, and Schloss Meseberg is a government guest house.",
  72: "Friedrich Merz became Federal Chancellor in 2025, leading a CDU/CSU coalition government. He is the leader of the CDU (Christlich Demokratische Union). Previous chancellors include Angela Merkel (2005–2021), Olaf Scholz (2021–2025), Gerhard Schröder (1998–2005), and Helmut Kohl (1982–1998).",
  73: "Following the February 2025 federal election, CDU/CSU and AfD are the two largest factions in the Bundestag. CDU/CSU won the most votes and Friedrich Merz formed a government. This reflects a significant shift in German politics compared to previous parliaments dominated by CDU/CSU and SPD.",
  74: "The Bundestag is Germany's federal parliament, directly elected by the population every four years. It is based in the Reichstagsgebäude in Berlin. The Bundesversammlung is a special body that only meets to elect the Federal President; the Volkskammer was the parliament of the GDR.",
  75: "Frank-Walter Steinmeier has been Federal President of Germany since 2017 and was re-elected for a second term in 2022. He previously served as Foreign Minister and is a member of the SPD. The Federal President is a largely ceremonial head of state elected by the Bundesversammlung.",
  76: "CDU stands for Christlich Demokratische Union (Christian Democratic Union), founded in 1945. It is a centre-right party and one of Germany's two major established parties alongside the SPD. The CDU operates in all states except Bavaria, where its sister party the CSU stands instead.",
  77: "The Bundeswehr is Germany's armed forces, founded in 1955 as part of West Germany's rearmament and NATO membership. It includes army, navy, air force, and other branches. Germany's police forces are separate and operated at the state level, not by the federal military.",
  78: "SPD stands for Sozialdemokratische Partei Deutschlands (Social Democratic Party of Germany), founded in 1863, making it one of the oldest political parties in the world. It is a centre-left party. It should not be confused with 'Sozialistische' — the SPD is social democratic, not socialist.",
  79: "FDP stands for Freie Demokratische Partei (Free Democratic Party), a liberal party founded in 1948. It champions civil liberties, free markets, and individual freedom. It has frequently served as a coalition partner for both CDU/CSU and SPD governments.",
  80: "The Bundesverfassungsgericht (Federal Constitutional Court), based in Karlsruhe, is Germany's highest court for constitutional matters. It rules on whether laws comply with the Grundgesetz, resolves disputes between state organs, and protects fundamental rights. It was established in 1951 and is widely regarded as one of the most powerful constitutional courts in the world.",
  81: "Under Article 63 of the Grundgesetz, the Federal Chancellor is elected by the Bundestag — not directly by the public. The Federal President nominates a candidate, and the Bundestag votes. This ensures the Chancellor has parliamentary confidence, unlike in presidential systems.",
  82: "The Federal Chancellor (Bundeskanzler/in) chairs and leads the Bundeskabinett (federal cabinet), which consists of all federal ministers. This is set out in Article 65 of the Grundgesetz. The Chancellor sets the overall policy direction (Richtlinienkompetenz) and the ministers lead their departments within that framework.",
  83: "The Bundestag elects the Federal Chancellor by absolute majority vote, as specified in Article 63 of the Grundgesetz. This is not a direct public vote — citizens vote for parties whose members then elect the Chancellor. This parliamentary system makes the Chancellor accountable to parliament, not just to voters.",
  84: "The Federal President's primary function is to represent Germany domestically and internationally — signing laws, accrediting ambassadors, and serving as a moral authority. Day-to-day governing is done by the Chancellor and cabinet. The President's role is deliberately ceremonial to avoid a concentration of power.",
  85: "The Bundesrat is composed of representatives of the 16 state governments (Landesregierungen), not elected directly by the public. Each state sends between 3 and 6 votes depending on population size. It allows the states to participate in federal legislation, especially on matters affecting them.",
  86: "The Federal President is elected by the Bundesversammlung (Federal Assembly) — a special body that convenes solely for this purpose. It consists of all Bundestag members plus an equal number of representatives nominated by state parliaments. This indirect election was designed to give the president democratic legitimacy without the populist mandate of a direct vote.",
  87: "The Federal President (Bundespräsident/in) is Germany's head of state. The current president is Frank-Walter Steinmeier. The Chancellor is head of government, not head of state. The distinction matters: the President represents the country symbolically while the Chancellor runs the government.",
  88: "The parliamentary opposition's constitutional role is to scrutinise and challenge the government, propose alternative policies, and hold the governing majority to account. It does not decide who becomes a minister or who sits in the Bundesrat — those are functions of the governing parties and state governments respectively.",
  89: "A Fraktion is a formal grouping of MPs from one party (or allied parties) within the Bundestag, requiring at least 5% of all members to qualify. Fraktionen have resources, speaking rights, and influence that individual MPs lack. The term is distinct from 'Partei' (the party itself) and 'Opposition' (the non-governing side).",
  90: "The Bundesrat is the constitutional body through which the 16 state governments participate in federal legislation. Laws that affect the states — especially on administrative or financial matters — require Bundesrat approval. This gives states a direct voice in national policy, unlike in fully centralised systems.",
  91: "If a state election changes the political composition of a state government, that state's votes in the Bundesrat may shift, potentially changing the federal majority there. Since many laws require Bundesrat approval, a hostile Bundesrat majority can block or delay the federal government's programme — making governing harder.",
  92: "CSU stands for Christlich Soziale Union (Christian Social Union), the Bavarian sister party of the CDU. The two parties form a joint parliamentary group (Fraktion) in the Bundestag and never stand against each other — CDU stands everywhere except Bavaria, CSU only in Bavaria.",
  93: "In Germany's mixed electoral system, the Zweitstimme (second vote) for a party determines how many seats that party receives in the Bundestag overall — more second votes means more seats. The Erststimme (first vote) determines which individual candidates win their constituency. Second votes thus determine parliamentary strength.",
  94: "The minimum voting age for Bundestag elections is 18 years. Some states allow 16-year-olds to vote in local (Kommunal) and state (Landtag) elections — Berlin, Brandenburg, and Hamburg among them. But for federal elections, the threshold remains 18 across all of Germany.",
  95: "Compulsory schooling (Schulpflicht) applies to virtually all children in Germany from age 6 until they complete a minimum of 9 years of full-time education. Education is a state responsibility, so exact rules vary, but compulsory attendance is universal. There is no compulsory voting, religious participation, or legal duty of silence for children.",
  96: "Holocaust denial (Holocaustleugnung) is a criminal offence under Section 130 of the German Criminal Code (Strafgesetzbuch). It can be punished by up to five years in prison or a fine. This law reflects Germany's particular historical responsibility and is part of broader laws against incitement to hatred (Volksverhetzung).",
  97: "In Germany, social insurance contributions (Sozialversicherungsbeiträge) are automatically deducted from wages for all permanent employees. These cover health, pension, unemployment, long-term care, and accident insurance. Child benefit (Kindergeld) and housing benefit (Wohngeld) are separate state transfers, not automatic deductions.",
  98: "If MPs switch parliamentary groups (Fraktionen), the numerical balance in the Bundestag changes. If enough MPs leave the governing coalition's group, it could lose its majority for votes and potentially its ability to govern. MPs in Germany vote according to their conscience but face political consequences for leaving their group.",
  99: "Germany's social insurance contributions are split roughly equally between employer and employee — both pay approximately half each. This shared funding model has been in place since Bismarck introduced the first social insurance laws in the 1880s. Self-employed people are generally not covered by this system automatically.",
  100: "Statutory social insurance in Germany covers five areas: health (Krankenversicherung), pension (Rentenversicherung), unemployment (Arbeitslosenversicherung), long-term care (Pflegeversicherung), and accident (Unfallversicherung). Life insurance (Lebensversicherung) is a private financial product and is not part of the statutory system.",
  101: "Trade unions (Gewerkschaften) represent the interests of employees (Arbeitnehmer/innen) in wage negotiations, workplace rights, and working conditions. Employers have their own associations (Arbeitgeberverbände). Germany's system of collective bargaining (Tarifverhandlungen) between unions and employer groups sets wages for entire industries.",
  102: "The Bundesverdienstkreuz (Federal Cross of Merit) is Germany's highest civilian honour, awarded to individuals for outstanding service in politics, business, culture, academia, or social work. It was established in 1951 and has several grades. The 'Vaterländischer Verdienstorden' was a GDR award, not a Federal Republic one.",
  103: "The 'Ampelkoalition' (traffic light coalition) gets its name from the party colours: red (SPD), yellow (FDP), and green (Bündnis 90/Die Grünen). It governed Germany from 2021–2025 under Chancellor Olaf Scholz. The term is widely used in German political commentary.",
  104: "Under German maternity protection law (Mutterschutzgesetz), dismissal because of pregnancy is illegal. An employer who knows a woman is pregnant cannot use that as grounds for dismissal. The other options — illness, lateness, neglecting work duties — can be legitimate grounds for termination if properly documented.",
  105: "Election workers (Wahlhelfer/innen) are volunteers who staff polling stations and count votes after polls close. Vote counting is done manually and publicly in Germany, with representatives from different parties present to ensure transparency. The results are reported up through local returning officers to the Federal Returning Officer.",
  106: "This is the same question as 105 — election workers count votes after the election ends. Approximately 600,000 volunteers serve as Wahlhelfer/innen at each federal election. Serving as an election worker is one of the few civic duties German citizens can be called upon to perform.",
  107: "The Bundestag is elected for a four-year term (Wahlperiode), as set out in Article 39 of the Grundgesetz. An early election can occur if the Chancellor loses a vote of confidence and the Federal President dissolves parliament — as happened in 2005 and 2025. Otherwise, regular elections take place every four years.",
  108: "Only German citizens aged 18 or over are entitled to vote in Bundestag elections. EU citizens living in Germany can vote in European Parliament and local elections, but not in federal elections. Residency alone does not confer voting rights — citizenship is required.",
  109: "Under normal circumstances, Bundestag elections are held every four years. The term can end early if the Chancellor calls a confidence vote and loses, allowing the Federal President to dissolve parliament. Germany has held early elections in 1972, 1983, 2005, and 2025.",
  110: "The Bundestag's legislative term is four years, matching the rhythm of most European parliamentary democracies. The Basic Law sets this in Article 39. This is the same answer as question 107 — both questions appear in the official test pool to reinforce this key fact.",
  111: "Publicly calling for the destruction of Israel (Aufruf zur Vernichtung Israels) is a criminal offence in Germany under laws against incitement to hatred (Volksverhetzung). Critically discussing or criticising Israeli government policy is legal and protected speech. Germany's laws reflect its particular historical responsibility arising from the Holocaust.",
  112: "Ballot secrecy (Wahlgeheimnis) is a constitutional principle guaranteed by Article 38 of the Grundgesetz — no one can be forced to reveal how they voted. Voting takes place in a private booth and the ballot is folded before being placed in the box. This protects voters from pressure or retaliation.",
  113: "In a federal election, the party that receives the most votes overall wins — there is no single winner-takes-all outcome, but seat allocation in the Bundestag reflects the share of second votes. No party needs a majority to 'win'; winning means getting the most seats and being able to form a government.",
  114: "Voting in German elections is a right (Recht), not a legal obligation (Pflicht). There is no compulsory voting law in Germany — citizens may choose not to vote without penalty. This distinguishes Germany from countries like Belgium or Australia where voting is mandatory.",
  115: "Aktives Wahlrecht means the right to vote (to cast a vote), as opposed to passives Wahlrecht which is the right to stand as a candidate and be elected. In Germany, the active right to vote is held by citizens aged 18 and over. The passive right requires candidates to be at least 18 for Bundestag elections.",
  116: "Having the right to vote in a Bundestag election is called aktives Wahlrecht — the active right to vote. This is distinct from passives Wahlrecht (the right to stand for election). The term 'aktiv' refers to the act of actively casting a vote.",
  117: "Parties must receive at least 5% of the nationwide second votes (Zweitstimmen) to enter the Bundestag — this is the Fünfprozenthürde. Alternatively, a party can enter parliament by winning at least three direct constituencies (Direktmandate). The threshold was introduced in 1953 to prevent parliamentary fragmentation.",
  118: "The Makkabi sports clubs are Jewish sporting associations with roots going back to the Zionist sports movement founded in 1898. In Germany today there are around 40 Makkabi clubs open to all people regardless of religion or background. They promote Jewish culture and identity while welcoming everyone.",
  119: "Free elections mean every voter can decide without coercion whether to vote and whom to vote for — no one can be forced to vote a particular way or punished for their choice. Freedom in elections protects both the decision to vote and the decision to abstain. This is guaranteed by Article 38 of the Grundgesetz.",
  120: "Germany uses a mixed electoral system combining majority voting (Mehrheitswahlrecht) for the 299 direct constituency seats and proportional representation (Verhältniswahlrecht) for the overall seat allocation. Voters have two votes: the Erststimme for a local candidate and the Zweitstimme for a party list. The Zweitstimme determines the overall composition of the Bundestag.",
  121: "The 5%-Hürde (five percent threshold) requires parties to win at least 5% of all second votes nationally to receive seats in the Bundestag. This prevents extreme parliamentary fragmentation with dozens of tiny parties, as occurred in the Weimar Republic and contributed to political instability. An alternative route is winning three direct constituencies.",
  122: "German elections are governed by five constitutional principles: allgemein (universal), unmittelbar (direct), frei (free), gleich (equal), and geheim (secret). These are enshrined in Article 38 of the Grundgesetz. The question asks about three of them: frei, gleich, geheim.",
  123: "The 5%-Hürde is the minimum vote share a party needs to enter the Bundestag — specifically 5% of all valid second votes cast nationwide. It is a formal legal barrier set out in the Federal Electoral Law (Bundeswahlgesetz). The rule was designed after the Weimar Republic's experience with extreme political fragmentation.",
  124: "The Bundestagswahl is the election of Germany's national parliament — the Bundestag — which represents the entire country. It is not a direct election of the Chancellor or the Federal President. The Bundestag then elects the Chancellor from within its membership.",
  125: "Regular elections enable citizens to change the government if they are dissatisfied — this accountability mechanism is fundamental to democracy. Elections do not force people to vote, preserve existing laws, or redistribute wealth. The ability to vote out an incumbent government is what makes political power ultimately rest with the people.",
  126: "Before every election, eligible voters receive a Wahlbenachrichtigung (voting notification) from their local Gemeinde (municipality). This card states the voter's polling station address and opening hours. Voters need it to vote in person, or can apply for a postal vote (Briefwahlantrag) using it.",
  127: "The 5% threshold exists primarily because too many small parties in parliament makes forming a stable government extremely difficult — as Germany experienced during the Weimar Republic (1919–1933). A fragmented parliament with many small parties can lead to political deadlock and instability. The threshold encourages consolidation around viable parties.",
  128: "Members of parliament elected by citizens are called Abgeordnete (members of parliament / deputies). In the Bundestag they are also called Bundestagsabgeordnete or MdB (Mitglied des Bundestages). The Chancellor, ambassadors, and Minister-Presidents are not directly elected by the general public.",
  129: "The Bundestag is the only federal institution directly elected by the German people. The Federal President is elected by the Bundesversammlung, the Chancellor is elected by the Bundestag, and Minister-Presidents are elected by their state parliaments — none of these are direct public votes.",
  130: "A valid Bundestagswahl ballot must have exactly one cross in each voting column — one Erststimme for a direct candidate and one Zweitstimme for a party. Ballots with too many crosses, unclear markings, or additional notes are invalid. Image 1 in the official test represents a correctly completed ballot.",
  131: "A Bürgermeister/in (mayor) is the elected head of a Gemeinde (municipality) — a city, town, or village. They lead the local administration and represent the community. They should not be confused with the head of a Bundesland (Ministerpräsident/in) or the head of a party.",
  132: "Ehrenamt (voluntary work) means working voluntarily and without pay for clubs, associations, charities, or community organisations. Germany has a strong culture of voluntary service — around 28 million people volunteer regularly. It is distinct from paid employment or military/civil service.",
  133: "Postal voting (Briefwahl) is a legal and widely used option in German federal and state elections. Voters apply for a postal ballot in advance and return it before election day. Voting by phone, allowing others to vote on your behalf, or children under 18 voting are all prohibited.",
  134: "Citizens can take collective action through a Bürgerinitiative (citizens' initiative) to campaign on a local issue such as preserving a bus route. This is a form of direct civic participation protected by the right of petition (Petitionsrecht). Contacting the forestry office or a sports club would be irrelevant to this goal.",
  135: "Trade unions (Gewerkschaften) in Germany represent employees (Arbeitnehmer/innen) in negotiations over wages, working conditions, and workers' rights. The largest union federation is the DGB (Deutscher Gewerkschaftsbund). Employers have separate organisations called Arbeitgeberverbände.",
  136: "The Arbeitsgericht (labour court) is the correct forum for employment disputes including unfair dismissal. Utility billing disputes go to civil courts, neighbour disputes to local courts, and traffic accident claims typically to civil courts or insurance. Labour courts are fast and inexpensive for employees.",
  137: "Germany has a dedicated court system for employment disputes: the Arbeitsgerichte (labour courts) at first instance, Landesarbeitsgerichte on appeal, and the Bundesarbeitsgericht as the highest labour court. These handle disputes over dismissals, wages, working conditions, and employment contracts.",
  138: "If dismissed unfairly (ungerechtfertigt), an employee in Germany can file a Kündigungsschutzklage (unfair dismissal claim) at the local Arbeitsgericht within three weeks of receiving the notice. This right is protected under the Kündigungsschutzgesetz (Employment Protection Act). Missing the three-week deadline usually means losing the right to challenge.",
  139: "A court case (Prozess) in Germany arises when someone is formally charged (angeklagt) with a criminal offence or when parties bring a civil dispute before a judge. Converting to a religion, having a different political opinion, or getting a car towed are not grounds for a criminal trial. The rule of law means prosecution requires a legal basis.",
  140: "A Schöffe/Schöffin is a lay judge — a citizen without legal training who sits alongside professional judges in German criminal courts and participates equally in decisions on guilt and sentencing. The system involves citizens directly in the administration of justice. Schöffen are selected from the local population and serve for four-year terms.",
  141: "Rechtsanwälte/Rechtsanwältinnen (lawyers/solicitors) are the only legal professionals entitled to give paid legal advice and represent clients in court in Germany. Judges decide cases; Schöffen assist judges; Staatsanwälte prosecute on behalf of the state. Only lawyers act for private clients.",
  142: "A judge's core function is to hear cases at court and deliver judgments (Urteile). Judges in Germany are independent (richterliche Unabhängigkeit) — they cannot be instructed by the government on how to decide cases. This independence is guaranteed by Article 97 of the Grundgesetz.",
  143: "Judges belong to the Judikative — the judicial branch of state power. Germany's three branches are Legislative (parliament), Exekutive (government and administration), and Judikative (courts). The separation of these powers (Gewaltenteilung) is a core principle of Germany's democratic constitutional order.",
  144: "The judiciary is also called the rechtsprechende Gewalt (the power that speaks the law / judicial power). The other two powers are the vollziehende Gewalt (executive — enforcing the law) and the gesetzgebende Gewalt (legislature — making the law). These three terms are the formal German constitutional terminology.",
  145: "Judges work for the Judikative — the judicial branch. This is one of the three branches of state power alongside the Legislative and Exekutive. The separation of powers (Gewaltenteilung) ensures that no single branch can dominate the others, protecting against authoritarian rule.",
  146: "A court procedure in Germany is called a Prozess (trial or proceedings). Civil disputes result in a Zivilprozess; criminal cases result in a Strafprozess. The word 'Protokoll' means minutes or record, not a trial; 'Prozedur' is a procedure or routine.",
  147: "A judge's work is Recht sprechen — literally 'speaking the law', meaning administering justice and delivering judgments. Judges do not govern the country, draw up plans, or enact laws. Their role is strictly to apply existing law to specific cases brought before them.",
  148: "The police (Polizei) in Germany are responsible for monitoring and enforcing compliance with laws — for example preventing crime, investigating offences, and maintaining public order. The military (Bundeswehr) defends the country. The parliament makes laws; the police do not. Surveillance of citizens without legal basis is prohibited.",
  149: "Denying the Holocaust is a specific criminal offence in Germany (§130 StGB) and is a clear example of antisemitic behaviour. Visiting a Jewish festival or playing sport against Jewish teams are normal social activities. Criticising the Israeli government's policies is legal political expression and not inherently antisemitic.",
  150: "A Gerichtsschöffe/Gerichtsschöffin is a lay judge — an honorary (ehrenamtlich) position filled by ordinary citizens without legal qualifications. They sit alongside professional judges in criminal cases and have equal voting rights on verdicts and sentences. This democratic element in the justice system dates back to medieval German legal tradition.",
  151: "The Berlin Wall (Berliner Mauer) was built by the GDR (Deutsche Demokratische Republik — East Germany) beginning on the night of 12–13 August 1961. It was constructed to stop the mass emigration of East Germans to West Germany through Berlin, which had been haemorrhaging the GDR's workforce and professionals. By the time the wall fell, around 140 people had died trying to cross it.",
  152: "The National Socialists (Nationalsozialisten) under Adolf Hitler held power in Germany from 30 January 1933, when Hitler was appointed Reichskanzler, until Germany's unconditional surrender on 8 May 1945. This twelve-year period is known as the 'Third Reich' (Drittes Reich) or the NS-Zeit (National Socialist era).",
  153: "8 May 1945 is the date of Germany's unconditional surrender, marking the end of World War II in Europe — known in German as Tag der Befreiung (Day of Liberation) or Kriegsende. Hitler had died on 30 April 1945. The formal surrender documents were signed in Berlin-Karlshorst on the night of 8–9 May.",
  154: "World War II ended in 1945 — specifically on 8 May 1945 with Germany's surrender in Europe, and on 2 September 1945 with Japan's surrender in the Pacific. Germany was then divided into four Allied occupation zones. The Federal Republic of Germany (West Germany) was founded in 1949.",
  155: "The Nationalsozialisten were in power from 1933 to 1945 — a period of exactly 12 years. Hitler became Reichskanzler on 30 January 1933 and the regime ended with Germany's defeat in World War II. This is one of the most tested historical facts in the Einbürgerungstest.",
  156: "Adolf Hitler was appointed Reichskanzler (Reich Chancellor) on 30 January 1933 by President Paul von Hindenburg. This followed the Nazi Party's strong electoral performance but not an outright majority — Hindenburg appointed Hitler in the belief that he could be controlled. Within months, Hitler had dismantled democratic institutions.",
  157: "When Hitler came to power in 1933, the Nazis rapidly established a dictatorship — suppressing opposition parties, abolishing democratic institutions, and centralising power. The Enabling Act (Ermächtigungsgesetz) of March 1933 gave Hitler the power to rule by decree without parliamentary approval. Germany was not a democracy, monarchy, or principality under Nazi rule.",
  158: "The 'Third Reich' (Drittes Reich) was a dictatorship — a totalitarian one-party state under Nazi rule from 1933 to 1945. It was neither a democracy, a monarchy, nor a council republic. The name 'Third Reich' referred to it as the successor to the Holy Roman Empire (First Reich) and the German Empire (Second Reich).",
  159: "Free elections (freie Wahlen) did not exist under National Socialism. The Nazis held sham plebiscites with no real choice and used intimidation to secure high approval ratings. All the other options — press censorship, arbitrary arrests, and persecution of Jews — were defining features of the Nazi regime.",
  160: "World War II lasted from 1 September 1939, when Germany invaded Poland, to 2 September 1945, when Japan surrendered. In Europe, it ended on 8 May 1945. World War I lasted from 1914 to 1918; the Vietnam War from 1955 to 1975; the Gulf War in 1990–91.",
  161: "The Nazi state was defined by a policy of state racism — the systematic classification, persecution, and ultimately genocide of people based on race. This ideology underpinned the Nuremberg Laws (1935), which stripped Jews of citizenship, and ultimately the Holocaust. Freedom of opinion and religious freedom were abolished, not promoted.",
  162: "Claus Schenk Graf von Stauffenberg was the German army officer who placed the bomb in the Wolf's Lair (Wolfsschanze) in the failed assassination attempt on Hitler on 20 July 1944. He and many other conspirators were executed after the plot failed. The 20 July plot is commemorated in Germany as a symbol of resistance to Nazism.",
  163: "On the night of 9–10 November 1938, the Nazis organised a nationwide pogrom against Jewish communities — destroying around 7,500 Jewish businesses, burning 1,400 synagogues, killing at least 91 people, and arresting around 30,000 Jewish men. It is known as Reichskristallnacht or Novemberpogrom. The year 1938 is the key date to remember.",
  164: "The events of 9 November 1938 are known as Reichskristallnacht or the Novemberpogrom — a nationwide, state-organised attack on Jewish property, synagogues, and people across Germany and Austria. Broken glass from smashed shop windows gave it the euphemistic name 'Night of Broken Glass'. It marked the escalation of Nazi persecution of Jews before the Holocaust.",
  165: "Konrad Adenauer (CDU) was the first Federal Chancellor of West Germany, serving from 1949 to 1963 — the longest serving chancellor at that time. He oversaw West Germany's economic recovery (Wirtschaftswunder), NATO membership, and reconciliation with France. He is often called the 'Founding Father' of the Federal Republic.",
  166: "The chant 'Wir sind das Volk' (We are the people) was a defining slogan of the Monday demonstrations (Montagsdemonstrationen) in the GDR in autumn 1989. Hundreds of thousands of citizens took to the streets in Leipzig and other cities to demand political freedom and reform. These protests directly contributed to the fall of the Berlin Wall on 9 November 1989.",
  167: "After World War II, Germany was divided into four occupation zones controlled by the four Allied powers: the USA, the Soviet Union, Great Britain, and France. These four nations are known as the Alliierte Besatzungsmächte (Allied occupying powers). Poland, Sweden, Italy, Spain, and Japan had no occupation zone in Germany.",
  168: "Japan was not an Allied occupying power in Germany — it was on the opposite side in World War II as part of the Axis powers alongside Germany and Italy. The four Allied occupying powers were the USA, Soviet Union, Great Britain, and France. Japan surrendered separately in the Pacific theatre.",
  169: "The Federal Republic of Germany (Bundesrepublik Deutschland) was founded on 23 May 1949, when the Basic Law (Grundgesetz) came into force. It was established in the three western occupation zones. The GDR was founded separately on 7 October 1949 in the Soviet zone.",
  170: "Under National Socialism, all political parties except the NSDAP (Nazi Party) were banned — the Law against the Formation of New Parties of July 1933 made Germany a one-party state. Free development of personality, press freedom, and protection of human dignity were all abolished, not preserved.",
  171: "The soziale Marktwirtschaft (social market economy) combines free market principles — prices set by supply and demand — with state intervention to ensure social fairness, a welfare safety net, and protection for workers. It is distinct from a pure free market (laissez-faire) and from planned economies like the GDR's Planwirtschaft. The concept was developed by economist Ludwig Erhard.",
  172: "The GDR (East Germany) was founded on 7 October 1949 in the Soviet occupation zone — the eastern part of Germany controlled by the USSR after World War II. The three western zones became the Federal Republic of Germany. The ideological division between the capitalist West and communist East defined the Cold War in Europe.",
  173: "West Germany joined NATO (North Atlantic Treaty Organization) in 1955, becoming a founding member of the alliance's expanded structure. Germany was not a founding member of the United Nations (joined 1973) or the EU (the EU as such was founded 1993 — West Germany was a founder of its predecessor, the EEC, in 1957). Germany was never a member of the Warsaw Pact.",
  174: "The GDR (Deutsche Demokratische Republik — German Democratic Republic) was founded on 7 October 1949 in the Soviet occupation zone of Germany. Both German states were therefore founded in the same year — 1949 — just a few months apart. The GDR existed until German reunification on 3 October 1990.",
  175: "After World War II, Germany was divided into exactly four occupation zones — American, British, French, and Soviet. Berlin itself was also divided into four sectors. This four-power division laid the groundwork for the eventual split into West Germany (FRG) and East Germany (GDR).",
  176: "In maps used in the Einbürgerungstest, the occupation zones are typically shown in this arrangement: Zone 1 = British (northwest), Zone 2 = Soviet (east), Zone 3 = French (southwest), Zone 4 = American (south). This geographic arrangement is a standard feature of the test — memorise it as British-Soviet-French-American.",
  177: "After World War II, Berlin was divided into four sectors controlled by the four Allied powers — American, British, French, and Soviet. This created a unique situation where a city deep inside the Soviet occupation zone was partially controlled by Western powers. The division of Berlin became the symbolic frontline of the Cold War.",
  178: "From June 1948 to May 1949, the Soviet Union blockaded all land routes into West Berlin — roads, railways, and canals — in an attempt to force the Western Allies to withdraw. The Western powers responded with a massive airlift (Berliner Luftbrücke), flying in supplies for over 2 million West Berliners. The Soviets eventually lifted the blockade in May 1949.",
  179: "World War II in Europe ended with Germany's unconditional surrender (bedingungslose Kapitulation) signed on 8 May 1945. The surrender was not conditional on any political arrangements — Germany accepted total defeat. This date is commemorated as Tag der Befreiung (Day of Liberation) in Germany.",
  180: "Konrad Adenauer was the first Chancellor of the Federal Republic, serving from 1949 to 1963. He was 73 years old when he took office and 87 when he left — the oldest person to serve as German Chancellor. Ludwig Erhard succeeded him; Willy Brandt served 1969–1974.",
  181: "On 7 December 1970, West German Chancellor Willy Brandt spontaneously knelt at the memorial to the Warsaw Ghetto Uprising during an official state visit to Poland. Without speaking, he asked forgiveness for the crimes committed against Polish Jews and all victims of Nazism. The gesture became one of the most powerful symbols of German Vergangenheitsbewältigung (coming to terms with the past).",
  182: "A synagogue (Synagoge) is the Jewish house of worship, study, and community gathering. It is distinct from a mosque (Islam), a church (Christianity), and a basilica (a large Christian church). The word comes from Greek meaning 'assembly'. Germany has over 100 active synagogues today.",
  183: "The Wirtschaftswunder (economic miracle) refers to West Germany's extraordinary economic recovery and growth during the 1950s. Under Economics Minister Ludwig Erhard's social market economy, West Germany rebuilt from wartime destruction to become one of Europe's strongest economies within a decade. By the late 1950s, unemployment had nearly disappeared.",
  184: "The State of Israel was founded on 14 May 1948 based on UN General Assembly Resolution 181 (November 1947), which proposed the partition of the British Mandate of Palestine into separate Jewish and Arab states. The resolution gave Israel international legal legitimacy. The Zionist Congress and other bodies supported the cause but were not the legal founding basis.",
  185: "The 'Iron Curtain' (Eiserner Vorhang) was the term coined by Winston Churchill in 1946 to describe the ideological and physical barrier between the Soviet-controlled Warsaw Pact countries of Eastern Europe and the democratic West. It divided Europe from the Baltic to the Adriatic. The Berlin Wall was its most visible physical manifestation.",
  186: "On 17 June 1953, workers in East Berlin and across the GDR went on strike and took to the streets, protesting against increased work quotas and the communist government. Soviet tanks crushed the uprising. West Germany declared 17 June a national holiday (Tag der deutschen Einheit) until 1990, when it was replaced by 3 October.",
  187: "The GDR (East Germany) used a black-red-gold flag — the same colours as the Federal Republic — but added a coat of arms showing a hammer (representing workers), compass (Zirkel, representing intellectuals), and a wreath of rye (Ährenkranz, representing farmers). The FRG's flag has no symbols. This emblem distinguished East German from West German flags.",
  188: "The Berlin Wall was built starting on the night of 12–13 August 1961. The GDR government, led by Walter Ulbricht, ordered its construction to stop the massive emigration of East Germans to the West via Berlin — over 3.5 million people had already fled. 1961 is the definitive date to remember.",
  189: "The GDR built the Berlin Wall in 1961 — specifically beginning on 12 August 1961. It initially consisted of barbed wire, quickly replaced by concrete. The Wall eventually stretched 155 km around West Berlin, with watchtowers, death strips, and armed border guards.",
  190: "DDR stands for Deutsche Demokratische Republik (German Democratic Republic) — the official name of East Germany from 1949 to 1990. Despite the word 'demokratisch' (democratic) in the name, it was a one-party communist dictatorship under the SED (Socialist Unity Party). It ceased to exist on 3 October 1990 when it was absorbed into the Federal Republic.",
  191: "The Berlin Wall was opened on the evening of 9 November 1989, when GDR spokesman Günter Schabowski accidentally announced at a press conference that new travel regulations would take effect 'immediately, without delay'. Crowds gathered at checkpoints and overwhelmed the border guards, who eventually opened the gates. This date — 9 November — is one of the most important in modern German history.",
  192: "Brandenburg is one of five federal states that were part of the GDR (East Germany) before reunification. The others are Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, Sachsen, Sachsen-Anhalt, and Thüringen. Brandenburg surrounds but does not include Berlin (Berlin is its own federal state).",
  193: "From 13 August 1961 (when the Wall was built) to 9 November 1989 (when it fell), Berlin was physically divided by the Wall. West Berlin was an island of West Germany surrounded by GDR territory. Families were separated, and crossing was extremely dangerous — around 140 people died attempting to flee.",
  194: "3 October is Germany's national holiday — Tag der Deutschen Einheit (Day of German Unity) — commemorating the reunification of East and West Germany in 1990. On 3 October 1990, the GDR formally ceased to exist and its five states joined the Federal Republic. It replaced 17 June as the national holiday.",
  195: "Sachsen-Anhalt is one of the five 'neue Bundesländer' (new federal states) that were part of the GDR before reunification. It was created in 1990 from the GDR districts of Halle and Magdeburg, roughly corresponding to the historical region of Saxony-Anhalt. Its capital is Magdeburg.",
  196: "Die Wende (the turning point) refers to the political transformation of the GDR in autumn 1989 — from a communist one-party dictatorship to a pluralist democracy. Mass protests, the opening of the Wall, and free elections led to the GDR's transformation and eventual reunification with West Germany. It was a peaceful revolution.",
  197: "Thüringen (Thuringia) is one of the five eastern federal states that were part of the GDR before 1990. Known as 'the green heart of Germany', it is home to Weimar — the city associated with Goethe, Schiller, and the Bauhaus movement, as well as the short-lived Weimar Republic. Its capital is Erfurt.",
  198: "Sachsen (Saxony) is one of the five eastern states that joined the Federal Republic at reunification. It is the most populous of the eastern states and its capital is Dresden. It also contains Leipzig — the city where the Monday demonstrations of 1989 were largest and most significant.",
  199: "The 'Stasi' was the abbreviation for the Ministerium für Staatssicherheit (Ministry for State Security) — the secret police and intelligence agency of the GDR. It was one of the most pervasive surveillance organisations in history, employing around 90,000 full-time staff and 170,000 unofficial informants to monitor GDR citizens.",
  200: "Mecklenburg-Vorpommern is one of the five eastern federal states that formed part of the GDR. It is Germany's most sparsely populated state, located in the northeast bordering the Baltic Sea. Its capital is Schwerin. The state was recreated in 1990 after the GDR's administrative districts were dissolved.",
  201: "The five states that were part of the GDR are: Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, Brandenburg, Sachsen (Saxony), Sachsen-Anhalt (Saxony-Anhalt), and Thüringen (Thuringia). They are often called the 'neue Bundesländer' or 'neue Länder'. All the other options in this question mix in western states like Hessen, Niedersachsen, and Bayern.",
  202: "The GDR was a member of the Warsaw Pact (Warschauer Pakt) — the military alliance of Soviet-controlled Eastern European states established in 1955 as a counterpart to NATO. The Federal Republic of Germany (West Germany) joined NATO in 1955. The two German states were on opposite sides of the Cold War divide.",
  203: "The GDR's economic system was a Planwirtschaft (planned economy) — the state centrally planned and controlled all production, prices, and distribution. This contrasted with West Germany's soziale Marktwirtschaft (social market economy). The Planwirtschaft ultimately proved uncompetitive and contributed to the GDR's economic decline.",
  204: "German reunification happened through the legal mechanism of the GDR's five eastern states (Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, Brandenburg, Sachsen, Sachsen-Anhalt, Thüringen) acceding to the Federal Republic under Article 23 of the Basic Law. The GDR did not merge with or conquer the FRG — it joined it. This took effect on 3 October 1990.",
  205: "When the five eastern German states joined the Federal Republic on 3 October 1990, they also automatically became part of all international organisations and treaties the FRG belonged to — including the European Union (then European Community). This was confirmed in the Two Plus Four Treaty and welcomed by the EU's member states.",
  206: "Stolpersteine (literally 'stumbling stones') are small brass plaques embedded in pavements across Germany and Europe, each commemorating a victim of the Nazi regime. The project was initiated by artist Gunter Demnig in 1992. With over 100,000 stones installed, it is the world's largest decentralised Holocaust memorial.",
  207: "The GDR was a member of the Warsaw Pact (Warschauer Pakt), the military alliance of communist Eastern European states under Soviet leadership, established in May 1955. The Federal Republic was in NATO. The DDR's military was called the Nationale Volksarmee (NVA) and was integrated into Warsaw Pact command.",
  208: "The Stasi (Ministerium für Staatssicherheit) was the GDR's secret intelligence and domestic surveillance service — equivalent to the KGB in the Soviet Union. It monitored not just dissidents but ordinary citizens, using a vast network of Inoffizielle Mitarbeiter (informal collaborators/informants). Its files, now archived, run to 111 km of shelved documents.",
  209: "The GDR's coat of arms featured a hammer (representing workers), a compass/Zirkel (representing the intelligentsia), and a wreath of rye (Ährenkranz, representing farmers) on a black-red-gold background. This distinguished the GDR flag from the identical-coloured FRG flag, which has no emblem. In the image-based test question, Image 1 shows this emblem.",
  210: "On 17 June 1953, workers across the GDR launched nationwide strikes and street protests, initially triggered by increased work quotas but quickly expanding into a broader uprising against the communist government. Soviet troops and tanks crushed the revolt, killing dozens and arresting thousands. West Germany commemorated 17 June as a national holiday until reunification in 1990.",
  211: "Willy Brandt (SPD Chancellor 1969–1974) is associated with the Ostverträge — a series of treaties normalising relations between West Germany and Eastern bloc countries, including the Soviet Union, Poland, and East Germany. His policy of Annäherung (rapprochement) through change, known as Ostpolitik, was controversial at home but earned him the Nobel Peace Prize in 1971.",
  212: "Germany's full official name is Bundesrepublik Deutschland (Federal Republic of Germany). This name was adopted when the Basic Law came into force on 23 May 1949. It distinguishes the democratic federal state from the GDR (Deutsche Demokratische Republik), which no longer exists since reunification in 1990.",
  213: "Germany has approximately 84 million inhabitants, making it the most populous country in the European Union. The population grew significantly after 2015 due to refugee arrivals and EU migration. Germany's population had been declining before this, owing to a low birth rate.",
  214: "The German national flag is black-red-gold (schwarz-rot-gold) in horizontal stripes — black on top, red in the middle, gold at the bottom. These colours originate from the uniforms of volunteer fighters in the Napoleonic Wars and became symbols of German liberal nationalism in the 19th century. The Nazi-era flag was black-white-red.",
  215: "Helmut Kohl (CDU, Chancellor 1982–1998) is known as the Kanzler der Deutschen Einheit (Chancellor of German Unity) for his decisive role in achieving German reunification in 1990. He worked closely with US President Bush, Soviet leader Gorbachev, and French President Mitterrand to secure international support. Reunification took effect on 3 October 1990.",
  216: "The Bundesadler (Federal Eagle) — a black eagle on gold — hangs prominently in the plenary chamber of the Bundestag in the Reichstag building in Berlin. It has been the heraldic symbol of German statehood for over a thousand years. The current stylised version was designed by Ludwig Gies and adopted in 1953.",
  217: "The GDR (Deutsche Demokratische Republik) existed from 7 October 1949 — when it was founded in the Soviet occupation zone — to 3 October 1990, when it ceased to exist upon reunification with the Federal Republic. It lasted exactly 41 years. Both German states were therefore founded in 1949, just months apart.",
  218: "At German reunification on 3 October 1990, exactly five eastern federal states joined the Federal Republic: Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, Brandenburg, Sachsen, Sachsen-Anhalt, and Thüringen. East Berlin merged with West Berlin to form a single Berlin state. This brought Germany's total number of federal states from 11 to 16.",
  219: "Germany has had its current borders since 1990, when the Two Plus Four Treaty (Zwei-plus-Vier-Vertrag) was signed by the two German states and the four Allied powers. This treaty confirmed the finality of Germany's eastern border along the Oder and Neisse rivers with Poland. Before 1990 West Germany had its current western borders but the eastern border was unresolved.",
  220: "27 January is Germany's official Holocaust Remembrance Day (Gedenktag für die Opfer des Nationalsozialismus). It marks the date Soviet forces liberated the Auschwitz-Birkenau extermination camp in 1945. The date was designated a national day of remembrance by President Roman Herzog in 1996 and is also the UN's International Holocaust Remembrance Day.",
  221: "The Schengen Agreement (Schengener Abkommen), signed in 1985 and implemented in 1995, abolished passport controls between participating European countries. Germany is a Schengen member, allowing its citizens to travel to most EU countries and several non-EU countries (like Switzerland and Norway) without passport checks. Not all EU countries are Schengen members — Ireland, for example, is not.",
  222: "Switzerland (Schweiz) shares a border with Germany along the Rhine in the south. Germany has nine neighbouring countries: Denmark (north), Poland and Czech Republic (east), Austria and Switzerland (south), and France, Luxembourg, Belgium, and the Netherlands (west). Switzerland is not an EU member but is in the Schengen Area.",
  223: "Poland (Polen) shares Germany's eastern border. After World War II, Germany lost significant eastern territories to Poland (the former German provinces east of the Oder-Neisse line). The current border was definitively confirmed in the Two Plus Four Treaty of 1990. Germany and Poland are both EU and NATO members.",
  224: "EU stands for Europäische Union (European Union), established by the Maastricht Treaty in 1993. It developed from the earlier European Economic Community (EWG/EEC). The EU is a political and economic union of 27 member states with shared institutions, a single market, and for most members, a common currency (the euro).",
  225: "Austria (Österreich) has the largest German-speaking population outside Germany, with around 9 million native German speakers. German is also an official language in Switzerland (alongside French, Italian, and Romansh) and in parts of northern Italy, Belgium, and Luxembourg. Austria shares a long cultural and historical connection with Germany.",
  226: "The EU flag shows 12 golden stars on a blue background — the circle of stars represents unity, solidarity and harmony among the peoples of Europe. The number 12 was chosen as a symbol of completeness, not as the number of member states. In the image-based test question, Image 1 shows this flag.",
  227: "Denmark (Dänemark) is Germany's only northern neighbour, sharing a land border in Schleswig-Holstein. It is both an EU and NATO member. The German-Danish border region has a significant Danish minority in Germany and a German minority in Denmark, protected by bilateral agreements since 1955.",
  228: "The accession of the GDR to the Federal Republic in 1990 is called Deutsche Wiedervereinigung (German Reunification). It was not an EU or NATO expansion — those are separate processes. The reunification was based on the eastern states joining the FRG under Article 23 of the Basic Law, not a merger of two equal states.",
  229: "Luxembourg (Luxemburg) borders Germany to the southwest and is one of the EU's founding members. It is one of the world's wealthiest countries per capita and hosts several major EU institutions including the Court of Justice and part of the European Parliament's secretariat. It is also a founding NATO member.",
  230: "Members of the European Parliament (MEPs) are directly elected by EU citizens every five years. The last elections were in June 2024. The European Parliament is the only directly elected EU institution — other bodies like the European Commission and Council are not directly elected.",
  231: "Europäische Integration (European integration) refers to the process of European states progressively unifying politically and economically through shared institutions — culminating in the EU. It began after World War II with the aim of making another European war impossible. It does not refer to immigration or emigration.",
  232: "European Parliament elections (Europawahl) elect MEPs (Mitglieder des Europäischen Parlaments) — the directly elected representatives of EU citizens. The European Commission is not elected but appointed. The EP has gained significant legislative power over the decades and now co-decides on most EU laws.",
  233: "The Czech Republic (Tschechien, officially Tschechische Republik) shares Germany's eastern border, along with Poland. It was part of the former Czechoslovakia, which split peacefully into Czech Republic and Slovakia in 1993 (the 'Velvet Divorce'). Both Germany and Czechia are EU and NATO members.",
  234: "Strasbourg (Straßburg) in France is one of the European Parliament's official seats — the EP holds its monthly plenary sessions there. The Parliament also has offices in Brussels (for committee work) and Luxembourg (secretariat). The choice of Strasbourg symbolises Franco-German reconciliation after centuries of conflict.",
  235: "The joint ceremony of French President Mitterrand and German Chancellor Kohl at Verdun in 1984 — where they held hands at the war cemetery — symbolised Franco-German reconciliation and the EU's core goal of lasting peace. Verdun was the site of one of World War I's bloodiest battles. Peace and security (Frieden und Sicherheit) between European nations is the EU's foundational purpose.",
  236: "The EU currently has 27 member states, following the UK's departure (Brexit) in 2020, which reduced the number from 28. The EU was founded with 6 members (France, West Germany, Italy, Belgium, Netherlands, Luxembourg) in 1957 and has expanded progressively. The most recent members to join were Bulgaria and Romania in 2007, and Croatia in 2013.",
  237: "The Treaties of Rome (Römische Verträge) were signed on 25 March 1957 by six founding nations — France, West Germany, Italy, Belgium, the Netherlands, and Luxembourg. They established the European Economic Community (EWG/EEC) and the European Atomic Energy Community (Euratom). The EEC became the foundation for today's EU.",
  238: "The European Parliament officially operates across three locations: Strasbourg (monthly plenary sessions), Brussels (committee meetings and additional plenary sessions), and Luxembourg (the Parliament's secretariat and administrative offices). This unusual multi-city arrangement is a result of political compromises between member states and is enshrined in EU treaties.",
  239: "West Germany joined with France, Italy, Belgium, the Netherlands, and Luxembourg to form the European Economic Community (EWG) through the Treaties of Rome (Römische Verträge), signed on 25 March 1957. These treaties are the founding legal basis of what became the EU. They should not be confused with the Paris Treaty (1951, establishing the coal and steel community) or the Maastricht Treaty (1992, establishing the EU).",
  240: "Euro banknotes and coins entered circulation in Germany and 11 other eurozone countries on 1 January 2002, replacing the Deutsche Mark. Germany had already adopted the euro as an accounting currency in 1999. The decision to give up the beloved D-Mark was controversial but supported by Chancellor Kohl as part of European integration.",
  241: "Elterngeld (parental allowance) is not paid automatically — parents must actively apply at the Elterngeldstelle (parental allowance office), usually located within the Jugendamt or a dedicated state authority. Applications must be made within the first few months of the child's birth. It replaces a portion of the parent's previous income for up to 14 months.",
  242: "Under German law, the decision whether a child attends nursery (Kindergarten) rests with the parents or legal guardians (Erziehungsberechtigte), not the state. While the state guarantees a legal right to a Kindergarten place from age 3, attendance is not compulsory. School attendance (Schulpflicht) is compulsory from age 6.",
  243: "In Germany, public demonstrations must be registered (angemeldet) with the relevant authority — usually the local police or public order office — in advance. This requirement is based on the Assembly Act (Versammlungsgesetz). However, spontaneous demonstrations in response to breaking events may be permitted without prior registration.",
  244: "The Abitur is Germany's university entrance qualification, equivalent to A-levels or the International Baccalaureate. It is completed after 12 or 13 years of schooling depending on the federal state. Without the Abitur (or the Fachhochschulreife for universities of applied sciences), students generally cannot enrol at a German university.",
  245: "A 13-year-old (Anne) cannot legally live with a 25-year-old (Tim) as a couple — this would constitute a relationship with a minor, which is illegal under German law. The age of sexual consent in Germany is 14, and additional protections apply for those under 18. All other pairings listed involve adults and are legally permitted.",
  246: "In Germany, the age of legal majority (Volljährigkeit) is 18. From this age, a person can enter contracts, vote in federal elections, marry without parental consent, and take full legal responsibility for their actions. Before 18, parental consent is required for most significant legal acts.",
  247: "Mutterschutz (maternity protection) is the legal protection afforded to pregnant women and new mothers in Germany, regulated by the Mutterschutzgesetz. It prohibits dismissal, limits working hours, and mandates paid leave starting 6 weeks before and 8 weeks after the birth. Elternzeit (parental leave) is a separate, longer entitlement available to both parents.",
  248: "Under German law and the Basic Law (Article 6), parents bear primary responsibility for raising their children (elterliche Sorge). The state has a subsidiary role — it supports parents but only intervenes directly (through the Jugendamt) when the child's welfare is at risk. This reflects the constitutional principle that the family is the primary unit of society.",
  249: "This is essentially the same question as 248 — parents (Eltern) are primarily responsible for raising children in Germany. The Grundgesetz Article 6(2) states: 'Pflege und Erziehung der Kinder sind das natürliche Recht der Eltern.' The state's role is supervisory and supportive, not primary.",
  250: "In Germany's labour market, educational qualifications (Ausbildung or university degree) are the strongest predictor of employment prospects and salary levels. Germany has a highly regarded dual vocational training system (duales Ausbildungssystem) alongside universities. Religious affiliation, gender, or party membership are legally irrelevant to hiring and are protected against discrimination.",
  251: "Germany outlawed all forms of corporal punishment of children, including by parents, in 2000 through an amendment to the Civil Code (BGB §1631). Children have a legal right to a violence-free upbringing (Recht auf gewaltfreie Erziehung). Parents who hit their children can face criminal prosecution and/or intervention by the Jugendamt.",
  252: "Germany only permits monogamous marriage — being married to more than one person simultaneously (Bigamie or Polygamie) is illegal under §172 of the German Criminal Code. Divorced persons may remarry; widows and widowers may remarry. Same-sex marriage has been legal in Germany since 1 October 2017.",
  253: "When moving home in Germany, residents must register their new address at the Einwohnermeldeamt (residents' registration office) within two weeks of moving in. This is a legal obligation (Meldepflicht) under the Federal Registration Act (Bundesmeldegesetz). The Standesamt handles civil status events like births, marriages, and deaths.",
  254: "Before a divorce can be finalised in Germany, couples must typically complete a Trennungsjahr — a full year of living separately. This separation year is designed as a cooling-off period and demonstrates that the marriage has irretrievably broken down. After the year, either spouse can file for divorce.",
  255: "The Jugendamt (youth welfare office) is the correct contact for parenting difficulties and family support in Germany. It offers counselling, mediation, and can arrange support services. The Ordnungsamt deals with public order; the Gesundheitsamt with public health; the Schulamt with school administration.",
  256: "Opening a restaurant in Germany requires a Gaststättenerlaubnis (restaurant licence / hospitality licence) from the relevant local authority (Gewerbe- or Ordnungsamt). This covers food hygiene regulations, alcohol licensing, and premises standards. No police permission, party approval, or registration office licence is needed specifically for this.",
  257: "Adults in Germany who want to obtain the Abitur can attend an Abendgymnasium (evening grammar school) or a Kolleg — institutions designed for working adults or those who didn't complete the qualification at standard school age. Universities require the Abitur for admission; Hauptschulen teach only up to secondary modern level.",
  258: "The Jugendamt has the legal authority to remove a child from a family if there is evidence of abuse, neglect, or serious endangerment — this is called Inobhutnahme. This is done to protect the child's welfare. The Jugendamt does not decide school placement, pay Kindergeld, or monitor nursery attendance.",
  259: "The BIZ (Berufsinformationszentrum) is a free vocational guidance service run by the Bundesagentur für Arbeit (Federal Employment Agency). It helps young people and adults find apprenticeships (Lehrstellen), explore career options, and access training information. It does not handle pensions, tax returns, or health insurance.",
  260: "School attendance in Germany is compulsory (Schulpflicht) — children must physically attend school regularly. There is no legal right to unlimited free time during school hours, no free choice of all subjects, and schools do not charge tuition fees (Schulgeld) in Germany's state school system. Home schooling is generally not permitted.",
  261: "An Abendgymnasium (evening grammar school) allows working adults to study towards the Abitur part-time, typically over 3–4 years of evening classes. This is a standard route in Germany for people who want to gain university entry qualifications later in life. Regular Hauptschulen do not offer Abitur; universities require it as a prerequisite.",
  262: "The Gleichbehandlungsgrundsatz (principle of equal treatment) means no one may be disadvantaged on grounds of disability, ethnicity, gender, religion, age, or sexual orientation. It is protected by the General Equal Treatment Act (AGG) and Article 3 of the Grundgesetz. Legitimate criteria like qualifications, experience, and salary expectations are not discrimination.",
  263: "In Germany, criminal responsibility (Strafmündigkeit) begins at age 14. Young offenders aged 14–17 are tried under the Jugendstrafrecht (juvenile criminal law), which focuses more on rehabilitation than punishment. Under 14, children cannot be held criminally responsible — they are treated purely as requiring welfare support.",
  264: "Rosenmontag (Rose Monday) is the highlight of the German carnival season (Karneval/Fasching/Fastnacht) — the Monday before Ash Wednesday. Huge parades take place in cities like Cologne, Düsseldorf, and Mainz, with participants in elaborate costumes and masks. It is a public holiday in the Rhineland.",
  265: "In Germany, civil marriage at the Standesamt (registry office) is legally required before any religious ceremony. A church wedding alone has no legal effect. The Standesamt registers births, deaths, and marriages. The Einwohnermeldeamt handles address registration, not marriage.",
  266: "Germany's statutory quiet hours (Nachtruhe) begin at 22:00 (10 pm) and last until 06:00 or 07:00. During this period, residents must avoid loud noise that could disturb neighbours — including loud music, power tools, and parties. This is regulated by local by-laws (Lärmschutzverordnungen) and the Hausordnung in apartment buildings.",
  267: "A 22-year-old is a legal adult (volljährig) in Germany, and adults have the right to choose their own living arrangements and partner without parental permission or interference. Parents have no legal authority over adult children's personal lives. This reflects Germany's strong protection of personal autonomy under the Basic Law.",
  268: "The German driving theory test (theoretische Führerscheinprüfung) is available in over 10 languages to accommodate people whose first language is not German. Language ability is not a requirement for obtaining a driving licence in Germany. The practical driving test is conducted in German, but interpreters can sometimes assist.",
  269: "Since 2013, every child in Germany aged 3 to school entry age (usually 6) has a legal right (Rechtsanspruch) to a place in a Kindergarten or equivalent childcare facility. The right was expanded to include children from age 1 in 2013. Local authorities are responsible for providing sufficient places.",
  270: "Volkshochschulen (VHS — adult education centres) are public institutions present in almost every German city and town, offering affordable courses in languages, culture, health, IT, and professional skills. They are primarily for adult continuing education (Weiterbildung). They are open to all age groups, not just youth or pensioners.",
  271: "Decorating a Christmas tree (Tannenbaum schmücken) is the central German Christmas tradition, typically done on Christmas Eve (Heiligabend, 24 December). Hiding Easter eggs is an Easter custom; wearing costumes is Karneval; placing pumpkins is Halloween. The Christmas tree tradition originated in Germany and spread worldwide.",
  272: "Polygamy — being married to more than one person simultaneously — is illegal in Germany under §172 StGB (bigamy). All other living arrangements listed are legal: divorced people living with new partners, same-sex couples, and single parents. Germany has recognised same-sex marriage since 2017.",
  273: "This is the same answer as question 255 — the Jugendamt is where to turn for parenting problems. It provides family counselling, mediation services, and can arrange support workers. It is distinct from the Gesundheitsamt (health), Einwohnermeldeamt (registration), and a doctor's practice.",
  274: "Opening someone else's mail intentionally is a violation of Briefgeheimnis — the confidentiality of correspondence — protected by Article 10 of the Grundgesetz. It is also a criminal offence under §202 of the Criminal Code. This right protects private communication from interference by both the state and other individuals.",
  275: "German divorce law requires that at least one spouse is legally represented by a lawyer (Rechtsanwalt/Rechtsanwältin) — this is called the Anwaltszwang. Unlike some countries, Germany does not require consent from parents, children, or a doctor to divorce. The process goes through the family court (Familiengericht).",
  276: "If you are treated poorly by a civil servant in Germany, you have the right to complain to the head of the authority (Behördenleiter/in). This is part of Germany's administrative law framework ensuring accountability of public authorities. Threatening staff is illegal; simply accepting poor treatment is not required.",
  277: "Refusing to hire a woman because she is a mother (or pregnant) is discrimination based on gender and family status, prohibited by the General Equal Treatment Act (AGG) and specific maternity protection laws. Legitimate reasons for not hiring — like lack of qualifications, experience, or unrealistic salary demands — are not discrimination.",
  278: "Refusing to hire someone because they use a wheelchair is disability discrimination (Diskriminierung aufgrund einer Behinderung), prohibited by Article 3(3) of the Grundgesetz and the General Equal Treatment Act (AGG). Lack of experience, skills, or unrealistic salary expectations are legitimate grounds for not hiring and are not discriminatory.",
  279: "A Hausordnung (house rules) is a binding set of regulations for tenants in a residential building, covering matters like quiet hours, use of shared spaces, rubbish disposal, and parking. It is typically part of the tenancy agreement. It does not list tenants, give directions to local offices, or regulate public transport use.",
  280: "If you disagree with a tax assessment (Steuerbescheid), you must formally lodge an Einspruch (objection) with the issuing tax office within one month. This is the correct legal remedy. Simply ignoring or throwing away the notice will not help and could result in enforcement action.",
  281: "Being refused entry to a public swimming pool because of skin colour is a violation of the right to Gleichbehandlung (equal treatment) — protected by Article 3 of the Grundgesetz and the General Equal Treatment Act (AGG). This is racial discrimination. Freedom of opinion, assembly, and movement are different rights not applicable here.",
  282: "Serving as a Wahlhelfer/in (election worker) is one of the few civic duties that German citizens can be legally required to perform when called upon by their municipality. It is an ehrenamtliche (voluntary/honorary) role but can be made obligatory. Other roles like club trainer, librarian, or teacher cannot be compulsorily assigned.",
  283: "An incorrect invoice from a German authority should be challenged by lodging a formal Widerspruch (objection) with the issuing authority within the stated deadline — typically one month. This is distinct from an Einspruch (used for tax matters). Simply returning the invoice or going to the Finanzamt would not be the correct procedure.",
  284: "Germany's economy and job market are changing rapidly due to digitalisation and automation. The recognised response is lifelong learning (lebenslanges Lernen) — adults must continue training and developing new skills throughout their careers, not just during initial education. Institutions like Volkshochschulen and professional training providers support this.",
  285: "Umsatzsteuer (VAT — value added tax) is not deducted from employee salaries. It is a consumption tax paid by businesses and ultimately consumers on goods and services. Employees do pay Lohnsteuer (income/wage tax), unemployment insurance contributions, and pension and health insurance contributions from their gross salary.",
  286: "The Betriebsrat (works council) is the employee representative body within a company, elected by the workforce. It has co-determination rights (Mitbestimmung) on matters like working hours, dismissals, and workplace policy. It is distinct from a trade union (Gewerkschaft), which operates across industries at a higher level.",
  287: "When ending an employment contract in Germany, employees must observe the Kündigungsfrist — the notice period specified in their employment contract or set by law. The minimum statutory notice period is four weeks. Failing to observe it can result in financial liability. The notice period is separate from salary terms, working hours, or insurance obligations.",
  288: "Germany acknowledges a special historical and moral responsibility (besondere Verantwortung) towards Israel and the Jewish people arising from the Holocaust — the systematic murder of six million Jews by the Nazi regime. This responsibility is expressed in German foreign policy, law, and education. It is not derived from EU membership, the Basic Law itself, or Christian tradition specifically.",
  289: "Refusing to hire a person because of their skin colour is racial discrimination, prohibited by the General Equal Treatment Act (AGG) and Article 3(3) of the Grundgesetz. Legitimate reasons for rejection — insufficient German language skills, unrealistic salary demands, or lack of experience — are not discriminatory. Skin colour alone can never be a lawful reason.",
  290: "If a product bought in Germany is defective, consumers have the right to Reklamation — making a formal complaint to the retailer and requesting repair, replacement, or a refund under the statutory warranty (Gewährleistung). Under German and EU consumer law, the statutory warranty period is two years. You cannot simply swap the device yourself.",
  291: "Germany levies a Kirchensteuer (church tax) — typically 8–9% of income tax — which is automatically collected by the state on behalf of recognised religious communities (mainly the Catholic and Protestant churches). When filing a tax return, declaring church membership determines whether this additional tax applies. Leaving a registered church (Kirchenaustritt) stops the tax.",
  292: "Religious tolerance (religiöse Toleranz) in Germany means that every person is free to hold any religious belief — or none — without state interference. This is guaranteed by Article 4 of the Grundgesetz. The state does not prescribe belief, nor does it require all residents to be religious.",
  293: "Painting or decorating eggs (Eier bemalen) is the central Easter custom in Germany, along with hiding Easter eggs for children to find. Decorating a Christmas tree is a Christmas tradition; wearing costumes is Karneval; firing rockets is New Year's Eve. Easter eggs are a pre-Christian symbol of spring adopted into Christian tradition.",
  294: "Pfingsten (Whitsun/Pentecost) is a Christian public holiday celebrated 50 days after Easter, commemorating the descent of the Holy Spirit upon the apostles. It is a two-day public holiday in Germany (Pfingstsonntag and Pfingstmontag). It is not a secular memorial day, a Bavarian tradition, or an international day of mourning.",
  295: "Christianity has been the dominant religion shaping European and German culture, law, calendar, and values for around 1,500 years. Major public holidays (Christmas, Easter, Whitsun) are Christian. Germany's Basic Law, while secular, acknowledges 'Verantwortung vor Gott und den Menschen' (responsibility before God and mankind) in its preamble — a nod to the Christian tradition.",
  296: "The four weeks before Christmas are called Adventszeit (Advent) in Germany — a period of anticipation and preparation. It begins on the fourth Sunday before Christmas. Advent calendars, Adventskränze (advent wreaths) with four candles, and Christmas markets (Weihnachtsmärkte) are all Advent traditions.",
  297: "Turkey (Türkei) is the country of origin of the largest group of immigrants to Germany. Around 2.8 million people of Turkish origin live in Germany today, a legacy of the Gastarbeiter recruitment programme of the 1960s and 1970s. Turkish communities have had a profound influence on German society and culture.",
  298: "The GDR admitted migrant workers primarily from Vietnam, Poland, and Mozambique through bilateral agreements with these socialist-aligned countries. These workers were called Vertragsarbeiter (contract workers) and had limited rights compared to citizens. After reunification, many faced uncertain legal status in unified Germany.",
  299: "Foreign workers recruited to West Germany in the 1950s and 1960s under bilateral recruitment agreements were called Gastarbeiter (guest workers). The term reflected the originally temporary nature of the arrangement — the workers were expected to return home after a period. Many stayed permanently and built communities, particularly from Turkey, Italy, Greece, and Yugoslavia.",
  300: "Italy (Italien) was the first country to sign a bilateral recruitment agreement (Anwerbeabkommen) with West Germany, in December 1955, marking the start of the Gastarbeiter programme. Subsequent agreements were signed with Spain (1960), Greece (1960), Turkey (1961), Morocco (1963), Portugal (1964), Tunisia (1965), and Yugoslavia (1968).",
  301: "Berlin's coat of arms shows a black bear on a white/silver background — the Berliner Bär has been the city's symbol since the 13th century. In the test image question, the correct image is Image 3 (Bild 3), showing the black bear. The bear appears on Berlin's flag, coat of arms, and countless civic symbols throughout the city.",
  302: "Pankow is one of Berlin's 12 official districts (Bezirke). It is located in the northeast of the city and is one of the most populous districts. Altona is a district of Hamburg, not Berlin — a common trap in this question. Berlin's other districts include Mitte, Charlottenburg-Wilmersdorf, Friedrichshain-Kreuzberg, and Neukölln.",
  303: "Berlin's state parliament, the Abgeordnetenhaus, is elected for a term of five years. This differs from the Bundestag (4 years) and some other state parliaments. Berlin held its most recent state election in February 2023 after the previous 2021 election was declared partially invalid by the courts.",
  304: "In Berlin, the minimum voting age for local elections (Kommunalwahlen) — specifically for electing the Bezirksverordnetenversammlung (district assembly) — is 16 years. This is also the case for Berlin's state parliament elections (Abgeordnetenhaus). For federal elections (Bundestagswahl), the minimum age remains 18 nationwide.",
  305: "Berlin's state flag (Landesflagge) is white and red (weiß-rot) — the same colours as the coat of arms (black bear on white/red). The design features two horizontal stripes: white on top and red on the bottom. These colours distinguish Berlin's flag from, for example, Bavaria's blue-white or Hamburg's white-red (but with different design).",
  306: "In Berlin, the Landeszentrale für politische Bildung (State Centre for Civic Education) is the official authority for political information and civic education. It provides non-partisan resources on politics, history, and democracy. While churches, consumer advice centres, and public order offices exist in Berlin, the Landeszentrale is the specifically correct answer for political information.",
  307: "Berlin is one of Germany's three Stadtstaaten (city-states) — federal states that are simultaneously a city. The other two are Hamburg and Bremen. Brandenburg is a regular territorial state that surrounds Berlin but does not include it. Berlin became its own federal state at reunification in 1990 when East and West Berlin merged.",
  308: "In the map-based test question, Berlin is shown as Image 4 (Bild 4) on the standard map of Germany used in the official BAMF test materials. Berlin is located in the northeast of Germany, surrounded by the state of Brandenburg. It is Germany's capital and largest city with approximately 3.7 million inhabitants.",
  309: "Berlin's head of government is called the Regierende Bürgermeisterin/Regierender Bürgermeister (Governing Mayor). This title reflects Berlin's dual status as both a city and a federal state. The current Governing Mayor is Kai Wegner (CDU). This is distinct from Ministerpräsident (used in regular states) and Erster Bürgermeister (used in Hamburg and Bremen).",
  310: "Berlin does not have a Senator for External Relations (Senatorin/Senator für Außenbeziehungen) — foreign affairs is exclusively a federal (Bund) responsibility under the Basic Law, not a state matter. Berlin does have senators for Finance, Interior, Justice, and other policy areas that fall within state competence. This question tests knowledge of the division of powers in Germany's federal system.",
};
